WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.270
Every Monday, half our class arrives late.

2
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:05.840
Our head teacher asked us why.

3
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.040
We had a guess: maybe we are

4
00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:10.200
tired after the weekend.

5
00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:13.470
But a guess is not an answer.

6
00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.780
So we asked the data.

7
00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:19.270
Today: what data science is,

8
00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:20.930
and how you can use it.

9
00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:24.480
Data science means using data

10
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:25.540
to answer a question.

11
00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:28.880
Data engineers collect and store the data.

12
00:00:29.510 --> 00:00:32.170
Data scientists ask it questions, find

13
00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:34.270
patterns, and explain what they mean.

14
00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:37.990
Data science is a loop with four steps.

15
00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:40.240
Ask a clear question.

16
00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:42.460
Collect the data you need.

17
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.120
Explore it with numbers and charts.

18
00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:47.660
Then explain what you found,

19
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:49.320
and ask the next question.

20
00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:51.820
Our teacher showed us the

21
00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:53.720
register, and we counted late

22
00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.220
arrivals for four weeks, day by day.

23
00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:58.690
Monday had twelve, the other

24
00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:00.050
days only about three.

25
00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:03.290
The pattern was clear, and it was not

26
00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:05.930
tiredness, because Friday was fine too.

27
00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:09.940
So we asked the next question: how

28
00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:11.540
do the late pupils travel?

29
00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:13.440
Most came on the same bus.

30
00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.500
On Mondays, it leaves the market late.

31
00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:19.310
The data led us to the real cause.

32
00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:23.500
A good data question is clear, you

33
00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.640
can measure it, and it is about

34
00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:27.020
something you can act on.

35
00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:30.030
Are pupils happy is hard to measure.

36
00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:32.510
How many pupils arrive late,

37
00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:34.750
on which days, is easy.

38
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:36.400
Quick check!

39
00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:38.720
Which is the better data question for your

40
00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:41.900
class: do we like reading, or how many

41
00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:43.520
books did each of us borrow this term?

42
00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:46.010
Books borrowed this term.

43
00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:48.050
You can count it from the library

44
00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:49.910
cards, and compare classes.

45
00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:53.670
Liking reading matters too, but you would

46
00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:55.750
need a careful survey to measure it.

47
00:01:56.830 --> 00:01:59.010
In the app, open Tidy tables.

48
00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.380
A messy sheet gives the wrong answers.

49
00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:05.630
Fix it step by step, and watch

50
00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:06.990
the answers become right.

51
00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:08.780
Your turn.

52
00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:10.790
We explained our finding.

53
00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:13.330
What comes next in the loop?

54
00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:15.330
A new question.

55
00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:17.810
Remember: data science

56
00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:19.590
answers questions with data.

57
00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:22.860
Ask, collect, explore,

58
00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:25.800
explain, and go round again.

59
00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:29.660
Try it yourself: write three questions

60
00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:32.380
about your school that data could answer.

61
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.180
Choose one, count the answer this week,

62
00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:37.220
and say where your number came from.
